From: cvediver <cvediver@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 7 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: qualcomm: ppe: software csum/GSO for
 multi-tagged frames

The EDMA TX path enables the PPE inline checksum/TSO engine for
CHECKSUM_PARTIAL skbs by setting the ip_csum/l4_csum bits in TX
descriptor word5 (edma_tx_fill_pp_desc()), but never programs L3/L4
offsets and ignores the skb's csum_start/csum_offset: the hardware
locates the headers with its own parser. That violates the
NETIF_F_HW_CSUM contract the driver advertises, and turns any frame
layout the parser cannot walk into silent L4 checksum corruption on
the wire.

A single 802.1Q tag is parsed correctly - that is why the rtl837x
switch tagging was moved to tag_8021q (DSA_TAG_PROTO_VSC73XX_8021Q)
and the previous blanket software-csum fallback for DSA conduits was
dropped. But the driver configures no outer-TPID/QinQ parsing (the
vendor SSDK exposes stag_tpid/qinq_mode registers for this; nothing
in this driver touches them), so a frame carrying two VLAN headers
is at best unverified and at worst checksummed at offsets 4 bytes
short. Double-tagged frames are a normal product of the tag_8021q
conduit path: a user VLAN sub-interface on a switch port, or
host-originated traffic through a VLAN-aware bridge, ends up as
tag_8021q outer tag + user/bridge inner tag on the conduit.

Restore a fallback in ndo_features_check, but a targeted one: strip
the checksum and GSO features only when the skb carries more than
one VLAN header (skb_vlan_tagged_multi()). Untagged and
single-tagged frames - including all plain tag_8021q conduit traffic
- keep full hardware checksum/TSO offload.

Note that defining ndo_features_check also bypasses the core's
dflt_features_check()/vlan_features_check() for this driver, and
even that default would not have helped: it deliberately keeps
NETIF_F_HW_CSUM for multi-tagged skbs on the assumption the hardware
honours csum_start/csum_offset, which this hardware does not.

Signed-off-by: cvediver <cvediver@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c | 17 ++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/qualcomm/ppe/edma_port.c
@@ -11,6 +11,8 @@
 #include <linux/phylink.h>
 #include <linux/printk.h>
 
+#include <linux/if_vlan.h>
+
 #include "edma.h"
 #include "edma_cfg_rx.h"
 #include "edma_cfg_tx.h"
@@ -177,10 +179,25 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
-static netdev_features_t edma_port_feature_check(__maybe_unused struct sk_buff *skb,
+static netdev_features_t edma_port_feature_check(struct sk_buff *skb,
 						 __maybe_unused struct net_device *netdev,
 						 netdev_features_t features)
 {
+	/* The PPE TX checksum/TSO engine locates the L3/L4 headers with an
+	 * inline packet parser: no offsets are programmed into the TX
+	 * descriptor and csum_start/csum_offset are ignored.  The parser
+	 * handles standard Ethernet with at most one 802.1Q tag (the
+	 * tag_8021q DSA conduit case), but no outer-TPID/QinQ parsing is
+	 * configured anywhere in this driver, so a frame carrying more
+	 * than one VLAN header - a user VLAN or bridge VLAN stacked on
+	 * the tag_8021q conduit tag, or plain QinQ - would have its L4
+	 * checksum computed at the wrong offsets and silently corrupted
+	 * on the wire.  Fall back to software checksumming and GSO for
+	 * such frames.
+	 */
+	if (skb_vlan_tagged_multi(skb))
+		features &= ~(NETIF_F_CSUM_MASK | NETIF_F_GSO_MASK);
+
 	return features;
 }
 
